# cuttle stealth-chromium patch #53 - persona BarcodeDetector surface.
#
# Source: chromium 151.0.7922.137
#   third_party/blink/renderer/modules/shapedetection/barcode_detector.cc
#   third_party/blink/renderer/modules/shapedetection/barcode_detector_statics.cc
#
# BarcodeDetector is exposed on the macOS persona only (via
# --enable-blink-features=BarcodeDetector), because it is the single feature
# separating CreepJS's Windows and Mac platform estimates - the other
# discriminator, hasTouch, is a tautology on the Windows side. Real Chrome
# ships the interface on Mac/Android/ChromeOS and nowhere else, which is why
# the Windows persona must NOT get it.
#
# Exposing it creates an obligation: the Linux container has no
# shape-detection backend, so the mojo pipe disconnects and the spec's error
# paths produce an EMPTY format list and a rejected detect(). "Interface
# present, but hollow" is a configuration that ships nowhere on earth - a
# stronger tell than the interface simply being absent. Same failure mode as
# the empty speechSynthesis list that patch #52 fixed.
#
# Chromium's own ZXing-backed provider (barcode_detection_provider_chrome.cc)
# is not an option: services/shape_detection/features.gni gates it on
# is_chrome_branded, i.e. the proprietary Barhopper blob.
#
# Known limits, deliberate: a probe that supplies a KNOWN barcode image and
# expects a decode will see [] where a real Mac decodes it, and detect()
# returns in ~0ms where real Vision takes measurable time. No detector
# observed in the wild reads either - of 20 deobfuscated commercial scripts
# (Akamai, DataDome, PerimeterX, Forter, WhiteOps, Sift, ThreatMetrix,
# FingerprintJS, Arkose, Distil, BotGuard) none reference the API at all, and
# CreepJS tests existence only.
